Let the browser's own pages be reached under the browser's own name

aerium://settings, aerium://flags, aerium://version and every other chrome:
host now work. chrome:// keeps working exactly as before. This is an alias, in
one direction, and the direction matters: aerium: is rewritten to chrome: on
its way into a navigation, and nothing rewrites chrome: back into aerium:.

Four pieces.

kAeriumScheme in chrome/common/url_constants.h, beside the other scheme
constants in that file, so the string is written once.

The scheme is registered as standard in chrome_content_client.cc. The comment
above that list says you only need to be in it to take part in the web platform,
and that non-special URLs now parse a host on their own. That is true today. It
is also a parser behaviour that has changed more than once, and this scheme has
exactly one job: aerium://settings has to split into a host and a path on every
platform and every Chromium this ships on, because the rewrite replaces the
scheme and keeps the rest. One entry buys that certainty. What it costs is an
origin that no document is ever committed in, because every aerium: URL has
become a chrome: URL before anything loads it.

ProfileIOData::IsHandledProtocol is what makes the omnibox treat
aerium://settings as an address instead of as something to search for.
ChromeAutocompleteSchemeClassifier asks that function first, and a scheme it
does not recognise falls through to the external-protocol check, which on Linux
and Android returns "search for this".

And the rewrite itself, registered first in BrowserURLHandlerCreated so that
aerium://newtab and aerium://about reach the handlers that know what those
hosts mean.

Two things about that handler are deliberate and would be bugs if they were
accidents.

It returns false even when it rewrote the URL. RewriteURLIfNecessary stops at
the first handler that returns true, so claiming the URL would mean
chrome://settings never reaching HandleWebUI, chrome://about never reaching the
about rewriter and chrome://newtab never reaching the NTP handler: the alias
would resolve the scheme and then skip everything that gives those hosts their
meaning. Mutating through the pointer and declining to claim the URL is how a
handler joins the chain instead of ending it, and HandleViewSource does the
same on its error path.

It has no reverse handler, and needs none. The navigation entry keeps the URL
as it was typed as its virtual URL - RewriteUrlForNavigation copies the
original before rewriting - so aerium://settings stays aerium://settings in the
omnibox and chrome://settings stays chrome://settings. Each spelling displays
as the user wrote it.

That last point is the deliberate limit on "replace chrome:// with aerium://".
Forcing every chrome: page to display as aerium: is a different change: it
would rewrite what users copy out of the address bar into bug reports and into
other browsers, where aerium://settings means nothing, and it would make the
displayed URL differ from the committed one on every internal page in the
browser. Offering the name and honouring whichever one was used is the part
that is unambiguously an improvement.

Nothing here lets a web page reach an internal page. A page that navigates to
aerium://settings is rewritten to chrome://settings and then blocked by exactly
the check that blocks chrome://settings today, because the rewrite happens
before the navigation is authorised, not after.

--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
@@ -864,6 +864,34 @@ GURL ReplaceURLHostAndPath(const GURL& url,
   return url.ReplaceComponents(replacements);
 }
 
+// Aerium: rewrite aerium://<rest> to chrome://<rest> and then get out of the
+// way. See the patch for why this is one-way.
+//
+// The return value is false on purpose, including when the URL was rewritten.
+// RewriteURLIfNecessary stops at the first handler that returns true, so
+// claiming the URL here would mean chrome://settings never reaching
+// HandleWebUI, chrome://about never reaching the about rewriter, and
+// chrome://newtab never reaching the NTP handler - the alias would resolve to
+// the scheme and then skip everything that gives those hosts meaning. Mutating
+// through the pointer and declining to claim the URL is how a handler joins the
+// chain rather than ending it; HandleViewSource does the same on its error
+// path.
+//
+// The displayed URL is not affected, and does not need to be. The navigation
+// entry keeps the URL as it was typed as its virtual URL - see
+// RewriteUrlForNavigation, which copies the original before rewriting - so
+// aerium://settings stays aerium://settings in the omnibox while chrome://
+// stays chrome://, and neither spelling is rewritten into the other behind the
+// user's back.
+bool HandleAeriumScheme(GURL* url, content::BrowserContext* browser_context) {
+  if (url->SchemeIs(chrome::kAeriumScheme)) {
+    GURL::Replacements replacements;
+    replacements.SetSchemeStr(content::kChromeUIScheme);
+    *url = url->ReplaceComponents(replacements);
+  }
+  return false;
+}
+
